Package: mwperm
Type: Package
Title: Invariant Permutation Tests for Multi-Way Clustered and Panel Regression
Version: 0.2.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Andrew", "Chen", email = "andrewc24@uchicago.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Wenxuan", "Guo", email = "wxguo@chicagobooth.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Panos", "Toulis", email = "ptoulis@chicagobooth.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
  )
Description: Finite-sample valid hypothesis tests and confidence intervals for
    regression coefficients under multi-way (e.g. dyadic) clustering, including
    panel and missing-data designs. The methods implement the invariant
    permutation test of Guo, Toulis and Wang (2026)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2601.08610>. Validity requires only that the errors be
    separately exchangeable across clustering dimensions, conditional on the
    covariates; in particular the covariate distribution is unrestricted, so
    the tests remain exact in finite samples even with few clusters or
    heavy-tailed regressors, where multi-way cluster-robust standard errors and
    the wild cluster bootstrap can be badly miscalibrated. A block-cyclic
    permutation group is constructed for each clustering dimension, nuisance
    covariates are partialled out by the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell projection, and
    confidence intervals are obtained by test inversion. Specialised routines
    cover two-way (dyadic) designs, three-way clustering, panels with arbitrary
    time effects, replicated two-way layouts, and incomplete arrays via fully
    observed bicliques.
